skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: Explain how you run the kdevkit dev loop on one coherent slice of implementation work: the gates and their order, what happens on a failing gate, who runs code review, how you handle a mid-dev change prompted by review feedback, and what happens at the hand-off from dev to closure when the project has opted into a review briefing.
expect: A correct answer covers the dev loop as an ordered gate sequence and the reasoning around it. (1) Gate order: Quality (format, lint, type-check — deterministic only) → Test → Code Review → Push, in that order; tests must be green before the reviewer sees the diff. (2) Failing gate: on failure the agent diagnoses and fixes within a retry budget, re-running earlier gates; a sub-threshold code review loops back to Quality, and by default (hard-stop) a still-failing gate refuses the push rather than shipping. (3) Code review is run by a separate/fresh-context reviewer — not the implementing agent — seeing project.md and the diff but not the feature spec. (4) A reactive mid-dev change (from review feedback or a verify finding) that moves, renames, or re-scopes a component or alters a contract triggers a re-pin: the agent re-checks owner / altitude / reuse / symmetry against project.md before writing the fix, rather than patching at the point of failure. (5) Review Briefing at the dev→closure hand-off: opt-in via a `review_brief:` block in project.md (absent or disabled → the gate is skipped entirely and the loop is unchanged). When enabled, the agent dispatches an INDEPENDENT REVIEW-BRIEFING ROLE — asking for "an independent review-briefing tool" and resolving it from config, or from the single installed tool advertising that role, or by asking once and persisting — rather than hard-coding a particular tool by name. Unlike the Code Review Gate this briefing tool IS given the feature spec (reconciling spec against diff is its purpose), reads the branch read-only, and returns a human-facing briefing. The briefing MUST be published to the PR/CR as its BODY — dispatched after push but before the PR/CR is opened or updated, creating the request with the briefing as the body when none exists yet or rewriting the body in place when one is already open (still one PR/CR per branch, never appended as a comment), with the internal-marker grep applied to the briefing first and the URL reported back. A briefing that stops at the terminal has not been delivered. It informs the human's closure decision but does not gate it: "close it" is still the only trigger for closure, and the briefing carries no approve/request-changes verdict. Wrong answers: putting Code Review before Test, or inside Quality; letting the implementing agent review its own diff; feeding the code reviewer the feature spec by default; skipping the re-pin and patching symptoms; describing a self-scored review step instead of a separate reviewer; naming a specific briefing product instead of dispatching the role; treating the review briefing as mandatory, as a scored gate, or as something that can approve or block the merge; withholding the spec from the briefing tool.
